.TH PRINTBIGINT 3 "2026-09-25" "BigNums 3" "Library Functions Manual"
.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
bigIntToString, bigFloatToString, printBigInt, printBigFloat \- convert dynamic numbers to decimal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <bignums.h>

int bigIntToString(const BigInt *a, char **text);
int bigFloatToString(const BigFloat *x, size_t places, char **text);
int printBigInt(const BigInt *a);
int printBigFloat(const BigFloat *x, size_t places);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
ToString functions assign a newly allocated NUL-terminated string through text.
The caller must free successful results; previous strings are not freed.
Failure leaves the text pointer unchanged. Inputs are never modified.
Printers write equivalent text to stdout without a newline and now return status.
Integer text has a leading minus for negative nonzero values; zero prints as 0.
.PP
Float conversion takes size_t fractional places and rounds the stored binary
value to nearest with ties away from zero. It includes a decimal point even
at zero places (2 prints as 2.). A negative nonzero value rounded to zero keeps
its sign. Integer carries across the decimal point are preserved.
.PP
Buffers are dynamically sized. There is no 10240-place or fixed integer-part
limit. Available memory, representable sizes and computation time still bound
output. More output digits do not recover lost input precision.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns 0, -1 for allocation failure, INT_MAX for an unrepresentable size,
-2 for a NULL text output pointer, or -3 for printer stream failure.
Allocation/range failure writes no output. Stream errors can leave partial
output; also check ferror(stdout). These functions no longer report through errno.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bignums (3),
.BR bignums (7)
